      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(reef): add timeout and bounded JSON reads to relay transport",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nThe Reef relay transport client (`extensions/reef/src/transport.ts`) calls `fetch` with no timeout. A relay that black-holes the TCP/TLS handshake or stalls before returning headers will hang `ReefInboxConnection.drain()` forever, silently killing the reconnect/backoff loop.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThis follows the same pattern as recent merged fixes that bound network operations (e.g., #106035, #105885, #102865). The change wraps relay requests in `fetchWithTimeout` (15 s) using the existing injectable `fetcher`, so tests can stub stalled responses. Bounded JSON response reads already landed on `main` via #106456 (16 MiB success / 64 KiB error caps through `readProviderJsonResponse`); this PR adds only the missing request timeout on top of them.\n\n## User Impact\n\n- Prevents silent channel death when the Reef relay becomes unresponsive.\n- No behavior change for healthy relays; a stall surfaces as a `TimeoutError` at the 15 s deadline and triggers the existing reconnect path.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- New test covers a stalled relay timing out after 15 s; existing main-side tests cover the bounded JSON reads.\n- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs extensions/reef/src/transport.test.ts --run` — 10/10 tests passed on the exact head.\n- Exact-head controlled-relay proof (real loopback HTTP+WS server, no fetch mocks): healthy pull succeeds; a stalled pre-header request aborts at 15080 ms with `TimeoutError`; a 16 MiB+1 JSON body is rejected with `reef.relay: JSON response exceeds 16777216 bytes`; after relay 500s the inbox backoff loop recovers, drains the backlog, connects the live socket, and receives the pushed entry. Full transcript in the comments.\n",
        "branch": "fix/reef-relay-transport-timeout",
